# EXTRA EVIDENCE FOLDERS FOR tools/hooks/evidence_guard.py
#
# Copy this file to `.claude/evidence-dirs.local` — WITHOUT the .example suffix — and put one
# folder name per line. That file is gitignored and must stay that way.
#
# WHY IT IS SEPARATE FROM THE GUARD. Two evidence folders are named in the guard itself
# because their names are already public in this repository. The TEST-DOCUMENT folder's name
# is not, and naming it here would publish it — the same reasoning that keeps the 93-pattern
# scan list and the corpus-descriptor list outside the repo (CLAUDE.md 5.4): the scanner
# ships, the list never does.
#
# UNTIL YOU CREATE THAT FILE, THE TEST-DOCUMENT FOLDER IS NOT GUARDED. That is a real gap and
# it is stated rather than papered over. `tests/test_evidence_guard.py` says so in its own
# closing output.
#
# Blank lines and lines starting with # are ignored. Matching is case-insensitive and is a plain
# SUBSTRING test against the whole command line — so a bare name is usually enough, and no
# wildcards are needed or supported.
#
# BUT A BARE NAME MATCHES THE WORD ANYWHERE IN THE COMMAND, WHICH IS SHARPER THAN IT SOUNDS.
# An entry of `skills` blocked every command whose text contained that word — including reads of
# `.claude/skills/`, which is where Claude Code requires a project skill to live and which holds
# no evidence at all. It fired three times in one session on commands that touched nothing
# sensitive (2026-08-24).
#
# SO: WHERE A FOLDER NAME IS ALSO AN ORDINARY WORD, GIVE A PATH FRAGMENT INSTEAD — and give it
# under BOTH separators, because commands here are written with either:
#
#   skills/legal-translation
#   skills\legal-translation
#
# That is a NARROWING, not a loosening, and the difference has to be proved rather than asserted:
# after any change here, drive the hook with real command lines and require BOTH arms — the
# guarded path still BLOCKED, the freed path ALLOWED. A change that only ever loosens is a
# removal with a better name. `temp/test_guard_narrowing.py` is the shape to copy.
#
# Example (invented, not a real folder):
# my-test-documents
